    "contentHtml": "<p>Welcome to another deep dive into web performance. Today, we are exploring a topic that every developer, designer, and marketer needs to understand to stay competitive in the digital landscape.</p><h2>The Core Problem</h2><p>When developers build websites on fast local networks, they often overlook the real-world conditions their users face. Mobile users on fluctuating 3G/4G connections suffer the most from bloated pages. A massive 4MB header image might load instantly on a Mac in an office, but it causes significant latency in the real world.</p><h2>Understanding the Metrics</h2><p>Core Web Vitals have fundamentally changed how we approach SEO. Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) measures perceived load speed. First Input Delay (FID) and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) measure interactivity and visual stability. Unoptimized images negatively impact LCP and CLS simultaneously.</p><h2>The Technical Reality</h2><p>Modern browsers are incredibly efficient, but they still have to obey the laws of physics. Downloading megabytes of data takes time. Decoding complex image formats consumes CPU cycles, which can block the main thread and delay interactivity. This is why aggressive compression isn't just about bandwidth—it's about CPU overhead.</p><h2>Final Thoughts</h2><p>The web is visual, but it must also be fast. By taking control of your image strategy, you improve your SEO, increase your conversion rates, and provide a fundamentally better experience for your users. Start optimizing today.</p>"
